icle_allocated false for that candidate, but still true for origin? Actually vehicle_allocated is per candidate; we could allocate vehicle_unit_alpha to another candidate only if that candidate does not have vehicle_allocated true. But origin_availability_alpha still has vehicle_allocated true; that doesn't prevent allocating to another candidate because vehicle_allocated is per candidate, not per vehicle. The precondition vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit checks that the vehicle is available (not allocated to any candidate?). Actually vehicle_available is a predicate indicating the vehicle is free (not allocated). There's no explicit lock that vehicle_allocated prevents vehicle_available. The allocate action removes vehicle_available and adds vehicle_allocated. The execute_schedule_dispatch adds vehicle_available but does not remove vehicle_allocated. So after dispatch, both vehicle_allocated and vehicle_available could be true. That would break the invariant but maybe allowed. Then we could allocate the same vehicle to another candidate? The allocate action requires vehicle_available ?vehicle_unit (true) and not vehicle_allocated ?candidate (for that candidate). So we could allocate vehicle alpha to another candidate (e.g., destination) while origin still has vehicle_allocated true (but vehicle_available also true). That would result in two candidates having vehicle_allocated true for same vehicle, and vehicle_available true. This seems weird but maybe allowed. However goal wants vehicle_allocated destination_availability_alpha vehicle_unit_beta, not alpha. So we need to allocate beta to destination, not alpha. And we want vehicle_available vehicle_unit_alpha at end (i.e., not allocated to any candidate? Actually vehicle_available true indicates free; but we could still have vehicle_allocated true for some candidate and still have vehicle_available true; but goal likely expects vehicle_available meaning free, not allocated. But we cannot guarantee.

